<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Run settings for the required live-recovery validation and evidence-gate lanes.

  XML forbids the double-hyphen digraph inside a comment, and the test platform rejects the whole file when it
  appears ("Settings file provided does not conform to required format"), which silently kills every required
  live-recovery lane before a single test runs. So the command-line switches named below are written without
  their two leading dashes: read "filter" as the dotnet test filter switch and "settings" as the dotnet test
  settings switch.

  TreatNoTestsAsError is the point of this file. `dotnet test` with a filter switch of "FullyQualifiedName=..."
  prints "No test matches the given testcase filter" and exits 0, so renaming or moving either lane's test
  turned a REQUIRED job into a silent no-op: the gate job still checked out, still downloaded the evidence
  artifact, ran zero tests, reported success, and semantic-release published with nothing evaluated. The
  architecture guard asserts the fully-qualified name appears in the workflow YAML, not that a test with that
  name exists, so it stays green through the rename.

  Note on this file's own history: while it was malformed the lanes did NOT fail silently. A rejected settings
  file makes `dotnet test` exit non-zero and those jobs went red. What kept it unnoticed is that the recovery
  lanes in ci.yml run only on `schedule` and `workflow_dispatch`, so ordinary pushes never executed them.

  Every `dotnet test` invocation in the live-recovery lanes must pass this file through the settings switch.
-->
<RunSettings>
  <RunConfiguration>
    <TreatNoTestsAsError>true</TreatNoTestsAsError>
  </RunConfiguration>
</RunSettings>
